    "content": "<p>Sometimes, replacing windows doesn't mean changing the character of a home. It means giving it a cleaner finish while keeping the details that make it feel like home.</p><p>For this window replacement project in the Bothell area, Sound View Window & Door installed some Andersen 100 Series windows. The project included a prominent bay-style window at the front of the home, along with additional windows in other areas.</p><p>The goal was a finished look that worked naturally with the home's existing exterior.</p><figure><img src=\"/wp-media/uploads/2026/10/a9cf284c-3198-4e53-9adb-c21033dc0f23-scaled.webp\" alt=\"\" /></figure><h2>Before and After: Updating a Bay Window</h2><p>The bay-style window is one of the most noticeable features in the before-and-after photos. Its shape, white exterior, and divided-light grid pattern all play a part in the home's traditional look.</p><p>The replacement windows maintain that style while giving the opening a fresh, finished appearance. The white frames complement the home's white brick exterior, and the grid pattern preserves the classic look of the original design.</p><p>It's a good example of how window replacement can refresh a home without making the result feel out of place.</p><figure><img src=\"/wp-media/uploads/2026/10/4510b40d-4e3f-49ef-b37a-e07d72d78a7e-scaled.webp\" alt=\"\" /></figure><h2>Why Andersen 100 Series Windows?</h2><p>For this project, we used Andersen 100 Series windows, which feature Fibrex® composite material. Andersen describes Fibrex as a material made from a blend of wood fiber and thermoplastic polymer.</p><p>The 100 Series is designed for homeowners who want a contemporary, low-maintenance window option without giving up a familiar, traditional appearance.</p><p>Some of the benefits worth considering include:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Low-maintenance frames:</strong> The composite material is designed to resist flaking, blistering, peeling, and corrosion under normal use.</p></li><li><p><strong>A clean, consistent finish:</strong> White frames create a crisp look that works well with a wide range of exterior styles.</p></li><li><p><strong>Energy-conscious design:</strong> Window performance depends on the specific glass package and configuration selected, but modern replacement windows can help improve a home's thermal performance when properly specified and installed.</p></li><li><p><strong>Style options:</strong> Grid patterns and different window configurations make it possible to maintain the architectural character of a home while replacing older windows.</p></li></ul><p>The right window depends on the home, the existing openings, and the homeowner's priorities. Product selection is only one part of getting a good result.</p><h2>The Importance of Proper Installation</h2><p>Even a well-made window needs to be installed correctly.</p><p>For this project, the installation plan called for removing the existing wood windows down to the rough openings, setting the new windows plumb, level, and square, and using nail fins. The specified exterior trim included brickmold and an applied sill, with flashing and trim details planned as part of the installation.</p><p>These steps matter because a replacement involves more than fitting a new window into an opening. Proper positioning, flashing, sealing, and finishing all contribute to how the window performs and how well it integrates with the surrounding wall.</p><p>Attention to those details is especially important in the Pacific Northwest, where homes need to handle frequent rain and moisture.</p><h2>A Fresh Finish That Fits the Home</h2><p>The finished bay window gives the front of the home a clean, cohesive appearance. The white frames work with the existing brick, while the divided-light pattern maintains a traditional feel.</p><p>That's often what makes a successful replacement project: the new windows look like they belong there, and the details around them are finished to suit the home.</p><p>Every project has different requirements. The best solution depends on the existing windows, the condition of the openings, the home's design, and what the homeowner wants to improve.</p><h2>Considering New Windows for Your Home?</h2><p>Sound View Window & Door helps homeowners throughout the Greater Seattle Area explore replacement window options and find a solution that fits their home.</p><p>Whether you're looking to update the appearance of your windows, explore lower-maintenance materials, or improve comfort and energy performance, we'll help you understand your options without the pressure.</p><p><strong>Thinking about replacing your windows in Bothell or the surrounding area?</strong> Contact Sound View Window & Door to discuss your project and request a free estimate.</p><p>We'd be happy to help you figure out what makes sense for your home.</p>"
