    "originalPath": "/andersen-windows-and-doors/bringing-light-in-while-keeping-views-out-a-guide-to-andersens-obscure-glass-options-for-seattle-homeowners",
    "content": "<h1>Bringing Light in While Keeping Views Out: A Guide to Andersen's Obscure Glass Options for Seattle Homeowners</h1><p>Living in the Seattle area means we have a unique relationship with natural light. When the sun breaks through the gray, or during those long, glorious Pacific Northwest summer days, we want every ounce of daylight flooding into our homes. At the same time, with homes nestled close together in neighborhoods from Queen Anne to West Seattle, privacy is a major priority.</p><p>So how do you maximize precious natural light without giving your neighbors a front-row seat to your daily life?</p><p>You don’t have to resort to heavy, dust-catching blinds that block out the sun. Andersen Windows & Doors offers a stunning lineup of specialty privacy-patterned glass options that obscure visibility while letting beautiful, diffused light pour into your home.</p><p>If you look at the Additional Glass Options Info Sheet below, you can see exactly how these unique textures distort the view of an object (like a vibrant red vase) while keeping the space feeling open, bright, and private.</p><p>Click the link below</p><p><a href=\"\">additional-glass-options-info-sheet</a></p><p>Here is how you can use Andersen's obscure glass styles to elevate your Seattle home design.</p><h2>The Andersen Obscure Glass Lineup</h2><img src=\"/wp-media/uploads/2026/06/image001-6-600x329.png\" alt=\"\" /><p>Andersen provides several distinct patterned glass textures, each offering a different visual aesthetic and level of privacy to fit your home's architectural style—whether it's a historic Craftsman or a sleek, modern new build:</p><h3>1. Satin Etch Glass</h3><p>Satin Etch is a favorite for contemporary Pacific Northwest architecture. It is a pearlescent glass that offers a smooth, frosted, and highly sophisticated look. It delivers a high level of privacy by thoroughly obscuring shapes, yet it allows light to share beautifully between spaces. It’s perfect for creating clean, minimalist bathroom windows or modern entryways that let in the morning light without sacrificing security.</p><h3>2. Cascade Glass</h3><p>Perfect for our rain-loving region, Cascade glass mimics the organic look of falling water or rain running down a surface. This textured glass adds an elegant, dynamic movement to your windows. Best of all, it can be ordered in either a <b>vertical or horizontal orientation</b>, letting you customize how the \"water\" flows across your design.</p><h3>3. Reed Glass</h3><p>Characterized by clean, distinct parallel lines, Reed glass (often called English Reed) offers a brilliant geometric distortion. It catches the light dramatically, making it a spectacular choice for transitional homes and modern townhouses alike. Like Cascade, Andersen allows you to specify a <b>vertical or horizontal pattern layout</b> depending on your home's aesthetic.</p><h3>4. Fern Glass</h3><p>If you love a more traditional, nature-inspired, or artistic flair, Fern glass (also known as Single Glue Chip) features an elegant, textured frost that looks like delicate ice crystals or organic fern fronds blooming across the glass. It breaks up the light beautifully while keeping the view completely private—fitting in flawlessly with classic Seattle homes.</p><h3>5. Obscure Glass</h3><p>Often referred to as standard patterned obscure glass (or Stippolyte), this option features a classic, tightly pebbled texture. It provides a timeless, uniform distortion that works incredibly well in utilitarian spaces like walk-in showers, powder rooms, or side-entry garage doors.</p><h3>6. Spraylite</h3><p>As captured in the <b>\"image001 (6).jpg\"</b> breakdown, Spraylite glass features a softer, water-droplet or subtle stucco-like texture. It provides a unique, gentle distortion that sits comfortably between standard obscure glass and the high-movement patterns like Cascade.</p><h2>Modern Customization: Glass Spacers for the PNW Aesthetic</h2><p>Privacy glass isn't the only way Andersen lets you customize your view. To achieve that crisp, high-end look popular in Seattle modern architecture, Andersen also offers premium <b>Glass Spacer Options</b>.</p><p>The glass spacer is the small bar that separates the panes of glass in your window. While standard windows use stainless steel, Andersen lets you choose between <b>Black</b> and <b>White</b> glass spacers to perfectly match your design intent:</p><ul><li><p><b>Sleek & Modern:</b> Black spacers create a crisp \"shadow line\" that complements dark window frames (like black or dark bronze) beautifully.</p></li><li><p><b>Bright & Clean:</b> White spacers blend seamlessly into lighter frames for an uninterrupted look.</p></li><li><p><b>Perfect Match:</b> If you add Full Divided Light grilles to your windows, the internal grille spacer bar will perfectly match your chosen spacer color (Black or White), creating a flawless, cohesive finish.</p></li></ul><h2>Where to Use Obscure Glass in Your Seattle Home</h2><p>While you can put privacy glass anywhere you want to soften the sunlight or obscure an unsightly view (like a neighboring house that sits just a few feet away), here are the most common applications for local homes:</p><ul><li><p><b>The Primary Bathroom:</b> Use Satin Etch or Obscure glass by the bathtub or in the shower enclosure. You'll get to enjoy natural morning light on those gray winter days without needing heavy window blinds.</p></li><li><p><b>Front Entryways & Transoms:</b> Bring light into a dark foyer using Reed or Cascade glass flanking your front door. It keeps your entryway bright while ensuring delivery drivers or visitors on the porch can’t look inside.</p></li><li><p><b>Daylight Basements & Home Offices:</b> If your office or bonus room faces a busy street, sidewalk, or alleyway, obscure glass filters out visual distractions so you can focus, all while keeping your workspace naturally lit.</p></li></ul><h2>Series Availability</h2><p>Andersen makes it incredibly easy to bring these privacy options to life across their product spectrum. Popular lines like the <b>E-Series, A-Series, 400 Series, 100 Series</b>, and various <b>MultiGlide or Folding doors</b> all support the Satin Etch glass and premium black spacer customizations.</p><p>Ready to swap out those dusty blinds for a sleek, permanently private view that embraces every bit of daylight? Check out the pattern chart in  and contact your local Seattle-area Andersen dealer today to see these beautiful glass samples in person!</p><p><img src=\"/wp-media/uploads/2026/06/additional-glass-options-info-sheet-1-464x600.png\" alt=\"\" /><img src=\"/wp-media/uploads/2026/06/additional-glass-options-info-sheet-2-464x600.png\" alt=\"\" /></p>"
